# a64-mini - the smallest AArch64 board.
#
#   rsemu run a64-mini --media firmware=fw.bin
#   rsemu run a64-mini -p part=neoverse-n1 --media firmware=fw.bin
#   rsemu run a64-mini -p ram=1G -p periph=0x09000000 --media firmware=fw.bin
#
# A boot ROM at the reset vector, DRAM, and one peripheral aperture. It models
# no product. It exists so `cpu.arm.a64` has somewhere to run inside a machine
# file, and so the exception vectors and the translation tables are exercised
# by a real board rather than only by unit tests.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There is no architectural reset vector, and that is the point
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# An ARMv5 core comes out of reset at 0x00000000 (or 0xFFFF0000 with VINITHI
# strapped high) because the architecture says so, which is why machines/
# arm926.machine can map its ROM at zero and stop thinking about it. AArch64
# says the opposite: the core starts at `RVBAR_EL1`, which is IMPLEMENTATION
# DEFINED - a pin, a fuse, or a hard-wired constant, chosen by whoever built
# the SoC. So `reset` below is a property of *this board* and nothing more, and
# a downstream SoC file changes it without changing anything else.
#
# Zero is chosen here because it makes the boot ROM's first word the first
# instruction, which is the least surprising thing a synthetic board can do.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which part, and why the board says so
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `part = "cortex-a53"`, handed to the core's `cpu` property below. That is the
# whole extension lattice in one line (ROADMAP.md 6.1.1): a Cortex-A53 is
# Armv8.0-A with FEAT_CRC32 and FEAT_FP and **without** FEAT_LSE, so `CAS` and
# `LDADD` do not decode on it and a guest that probes `ID_AA64ISAR0_EL1` is
# told so. `-p part=neoverse-n1` is an Armv8.2 part where all three exist. Same
# binary, same core, different instance: the difference is a construction
# property and not a build flag, which is what lets one machine run two
# different parts side by side.
#
# `-p part=armv8.0` is the lower bound and has no optional feature at all,
# floating point included -- FEAT_FP really is optional in Armv8.0-A even
# though every part anybody ships has it, so it is the configuration that
# proves the ID_AA64PFR0_EL1 gate.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The space is 64 bits and the addresses are PHYSICAL
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# An AArch64 core translates before it reaches the bus, so what this file maps
# is physical addresses; the virtual layout is the guest's business and it
# builds its own translation tables to express it. Out of reset the MMU is off
# (`SCTLR_EL1.M` is architecturally zero), so the first instructions run on
# physical addresses and firmware that never touches `TTBR0_EL1` sees exactly
# this map.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nothing outside the core raises an interrupt -- but the timer inside it does
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The core has `cpu.irq` and `cpu.fiq` inputs, which a real board drives from a
# GIC. There is no GIC on this board and no other interrupt source, so neither
# is wired: a net with no driver would be two lines saying nothing.
#
# The **generic timer** is not one of those inputs. It is part of the core, the
# way the `PMU` and the vector table are, and its output goes to `IRQ` inside
# the core rather than across the board. On a real SoC it would be a private
# peripheral interrupt into a GIC and the GIC would drive `nIRQ`; with no GIC
# there is nothing between the two ends of that wire, so a guest here arms
# `CNTP_CTL_EL0`, clears `PSTATE.I` and takes an interrupt into its own vector
# table with nothing else on the board moving. That is what
# tests/a64_mini_board.rs does, along with `SVC` and `BRK`.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What the counter counts, and why the board owes two numbers
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `CNTPCT_EL0` advances in virtual time, and the virtual time this core owns is
# its own domain's tick counter -- one tick per bus access, on `sysclk`. So the
# board says how many of those make one count (`cntdiv`) and what to tell the
# guest that rate is in hertz (`cntfrq`), and the two have to agree: `cntfrq *
# cntdiv` is the frequency of the domain the core is on.
#
# They agree here because only one of them is written down. `cntdiv` is
# *derived* -- `cpu-hz / cntfrq` -- so changing either parameter keeps the pair
# consistent, and a value that does not divide exactly is a board that has to
# be looked at rather than a counter that quietly runs slow.
#
# 100 MHz is a real number rather than a round one: it is in the range Arm
# recommends for the system counter (1 to 50 MHz nominal, higher permitted) and
# is what several Armv8 SoCs actually use. Nothing architectural depends on it,
# which is exactly why the board rather than the core decides it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Floating point starts switched off, and that is the architecture
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `CPACR_EL1.FPEN` resets to zero on every AArch64 part, so the first `FADD` a
# guest executes takes a synchronous exception with `ESR_EL1.EC == 0x07` until
# firmware writes `CPACR_EL1`. Firmware for this board therefore starts with
# something like `mov x0, #(3 << 20); msr cpacr_el1, x0; isb`. It is not a
# quirk of this file: it is how a kernel finds out that a process has begun
# using the FPU.

machine "a64-mini" {
  # Which part the core is. `armv8.0` is a bare Armv8.0-A with no optional
  # feature at all -- floating point included, so an `FADD` is UNDEFINED on it;
  # `cortex-a53` and `cortex-a72` add FEAT_CRC32 and FEAT_FP; `neoverse-n1` is
  # Armv8.2 and adds FEAT_LSE on top.
  #
  # Named `part` rather than `cpu` because the *object* below is called `cpu`,
  # and the machine language refuses a name that is both a parameter and an
  # instance rather than silently picking one.
  param part = "cortex-a53"

  # Where the core starts fetching, which on AArch64 is the board's decision
  # rather than the architecture's - see above.
  param reset = 0x00000000

  # How big the boot ROM is. It is mapped at `reset` because that is where the
  # first instruction has to be.
  param rom = 1M

  # Where DRAM starts and how much there is. 0x40000000 is the conventional
  # place for it on an AArch64 board with a 1 GiB hole below for devices, which
  # is the reason for that number rather than a round one chosen for looks.
  param ram-base = 0x40000000
  param ram      = 128M

  # The peripheral aperture: where a SoC's register blocks live, and how much
  # address space they get.
  param periph      = 0x09000000
  param periph-size = 64K

  # How fast the core's clock domain runs, and how fast the generic timer's
  # system counter runs. See the note above: `cntdiv` is derived from the pair
  # rather than written down, so they cannot drift apart.
  param cpu-hz = 1000000000
  param cntfrq = 100000000

  # -- the crystal -----------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # One oscillator: everything on this board descends from the same can, so
  # every ratio on it is exact (ROADMAP.md 4.2) -- the generic timer's counter
  # included, which is a plain integer divisor of the core's own ticks and
  # therefore exact by construction rather than by accumulating a residual.
  # Arm defines no instruction timing at all, so this sets the wall-clock rate
  # and nothing architectural.
  osc sysclk = cpu-hz Hz

  # -- the bus ---------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # One flat 64-bit physical space. `unassigned` is left at its default of
  # `fault`, so an access to a hole becomes an external-abort data abort the
  # guest can see rather than a plausible-looking zero - which is honest here,
  # because an AArch64 core really does report one and `cpu.arm.a64` raises it.
  space mem { width = 64 }

  # -- the core --------------------------------------------------------------
  object cpu "cpu.arm.a64" {
    clock  = sysclk
    space  = mem
    engine = "interp"
    cpu    = part
    reset  = reset
    # What MPIDR_EL1 reports. Bit 31 is RES1 and the low byte is Aff0, so this
    # is "processor 0 of cluster 0" - the value a single-core board must give,
    # and the one an SMP guest reads to tell cores apart.
    mpidr  = 0x80000000
    # The generic timer. `cntfrq` is only what `CNTFRQ_EL0` reports -- on real
    # silicon firmware writes that register and nothing checks it -- while
    # `cntdiv` is what the counter actually does.
    cntfrq = cntfrq
    cntdiv = cpu-hz / cntfrq
  }

  # -- memory ----------------------------------------------------------------
  #
  # `image` names a media slot: whoever runs the machine binds the bytes
  # (`--media firmware=fw.bin`), and nothing below host/ opens a file.
  object boot "rom" { size = rom, image = "firmware" }

  object dram "ram" { size = ram }

  # The peripheral aperture, backed by plain memory. A SoC file replaces this
  # one object with its own register blocks at their own offsets; until then it
  # is a window firmware can write to and a test can read back, which is what
  # makes this board provable rather than decorative.
  object regs "ram" { size = periph-size }

  # -- the memory map --------------------------------------------------------
  map mem reset     size rom         = boot
  map mem periph    size periph-size = regs
  map mem ram-base  size ram         = dram
}
